  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Fante\"\nsubtitle: \"Ghana\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | Fante communities, part of Ghana's Akan-speaking world. |\n| Where | The coast and nearby inland areas of south-central Ghana. |\n| Known for | Asafo company flags ? Carved figures ? Terracotta funerary objects ? Drums |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Fante live along Ghana's coast and in neighboring inland areas. Their *asafo* companies have civic, political, and ceremonial roles. Each company can have its own insignia, including appliqu?d flags whose figures and colors make claims about identity and power. The flag motifs are not a single shared pattern: their meanings depend on the company and its history.\n\n## Material culture\n\nThe museum selection is strongest in *asafo* flags. Several show a Union Jack in the corner, placing them in the era before Ghanaian independence; others carry animals, people, or proverbs in sewn cloth. The British Museum identifies one elephant-and-palm flag as Fante work and explains its image as an assertion of strength. Two Brooklyn Museum flags add different company images.\n\nCarved *akua'ba* figures form a second group. The British Museum attributes the selected rectangular-headed figures to Fante makers and notes that older labels sometimes confused them with Asante examples. Terracotta heads and vessels from the Agona Swedru area show another material tradition. Their excavated funerary context is more specific than a claim that all Fante pottery served the same purpose.\n\nThe collection also has carved drums, a staff, a ceremonial paddle, and two historical portraits explicitly captioned Fante or Fanti. These older museum holdings document particular objects and sitters; they do not represent all present-day Fante life.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Asafo* ? A company with civic, political, and ceremonial roles.\n- *Frankaa* ? An *asafo* company flag.\n- *Akua'ba* ? A carved figure; the selected Fante examples have tall, rectangular heads.\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- [Smithsonian National Museum of African Art: Asafo flag](https://africa.si.edu/collection/object/nmafa_88-9-1) ? company organization, flag making, and imagery.\n- [British Museum: Asafo flag Af1978,22.715](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1978-22-715) ? Fante maker attribution and the elephant-and-palm design.\n- [Brooklyn Museum: Asafo Company Flag](https://www.brooklynmuseum.org/opencollection/objects/188917) ? a Fante flag and its political imagery.\n- [British Museum: Fante akua'ba](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af-8006) ? a carved figure and its documented attribution.\n- [British Museum: pottery from Agona Swedru](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1933-1202-11) ? provenance of the excavated pottery and funerary objects.\n- [British Museum: Fante drum Af1954,23.703](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1954-23-703) ? a selected carved drum.\n",
